# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-12, found by a judge in panel 034 while compiling something
# else. Symptom: `heroes test examples/calculator/lex.hero` answered `panic: 1 heap
# blocks still live at exit (a missing decref)` and failed a test that PASSED when the
# same module was compiled as part of a larger program — byte-identical source, four
# roots, one of them wrong.
#
# Cause, and it is the sharp part: `emit/perfn.rs::owns_case` asked the type interner
# whether a variant case's payload owns a reference. `Ty::Case(decl, case)` is interned
# when a `match` arm BINDS the payload — not when the case is built. So a module that
# constructs `.name(s: …)` and never destructures it emitted
#
#     void h_lex_Token_release(h_lex_Token *v) { switch (v->tag) { default: break; } }
#
# an empty switch, while the very same declaration compiled beside a module holding the
# `match` emitted the walking release. The retain was always emitted, so every copy
# increfed and nothing decrefed. Fix: `case_fields_are_counted` asks the FIELDS, which
# do not move between compilations, where the interner does.
#
# This program is that shape with nothing else in it: a case payload that owns a `str`,
# built and copied and never matched on. The `str` must be computed — a literal is a
# static block and cannot leak, which is why the corpus had 90 run cases and not one of
# them met this.
#
# What makes it a test rather than an anecdote is the leak counter in the shim: exit 0
# and correct output prove nothing here, because the defect produced both.

variant Token
    num
        v: int
    name
        s: str

# Never destructured anywhere in this file: that is the condition, not an omission.
function label(text: str, from: int, to: int) -> Token
    return .name(s: text.slice(from: from, to: to))

function main()
    out: [Token] @ []
    out @ out.push(label("alphabet", from: 0, to: 5))
    out @ out.push(.num(v: 12))

    # A second reference to the same payload, so a release that runs once too few is
    # as visible as one that never runs.
    copy = out
    out @ out.push(label("betamax", from: 0, to: 4))

    print(out.len())
    print(copy.len())

    # The whole point of the shape: `==` on a variant carrying a computed `str`, which
    # is what the calculator's own test does and what leaked.
    print(out[0] == label("alphabetical", from: 0, to: 5))
    print(out[0] == .num(v: 12))
